`std::string` not found in bench_dsa_kpool.hip line 48. Under hipcc, `<hip/hip_runtime.h>` transitively pulls in `<string>` (real HIP headers include lots of C++ std headers). CUDA's `cuda_runtime.h` doesn't. The bench file must rely on transitive includes.

Two options:
1. Patch the bench file to include <string> (and <cstring> for strstr — strstr is used! and <cstdio> for printf, <cstdlib> for exit) — the honest fix: the bench file should be self-sufficient. Under HIP it compiled because hip_runtime.h dragged in everything.
2. Add `#include <string>` etc. to the SHIM — keeping source files untouched. The shim's job is compat; pulling std headers that real HIP provides is legitimate shim behavior.

Which files are affected? The bench (std::string, std::strstr, printf, exit). The test compiled fine apparently (no error reported before bench failed? The build ran test TU first? No — order: dsa_kpool.hip, then test, then bench. The error shows only bench failed... but nvcc stops at first error per TU; the kernel TU passed (no error line), test TU passed (no error), bench failed. Wait — actually if dsa_kpool.hip or test had errors they'd appear. Only bench errored ✓.

Fix approach: add the std includes to the bench file itself? That changes repo source (fine — it's a portability improvement, real HIP just masked it). But minimal-churn approach: shim includes <string> <cstring> <cstdio> <cstdlib>. The shim already exists to absorb exactly this kind of difference ("maps the small HIP API surface"). Real hip_runtime.h does include these transitively. I'll add them to the shim — safer than touching the bench file, and consistent with the shim's stated purpose.

Add to shim after #include <cuda_runtime.h>:
```c
// The real HIP runtime header pulls in the C++ std headers its users rely
// on; cuda_runtime.h does not, so provide the ones vkernels sources use.
#include <string>
#include <cstring>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
```

Then re-run just the build (resync shim, resubmit job).

